    summary: 'A CUDA kernel is a function compiled for the GPU and marked `__global__`. One launch creates a grid of thread blocks, and every thread runs the same code with a different `(blockIdx, threadIdx)` so it can pick its own slice of the data. Kernels are the unit the runtime queues on a stream, the unit profilers time, and the unit that compilers such as Triton and Inductor generate.',
    purpose: 'Expresses data-parallel work in a form the GPU can distribute across its SMs without a host-side loop, and gives the runtime a discrete unit to queue, order and time.',
    mechanics: 'Source is split by `nvcc` into host code (compiled by the host C++ compiler) and device code (compiled to PTX and SASS and embedded in the binary as a fat binary). The host calls `kernel<<<grid, block, sharedBytes, stream>>>(args)`, which the runtime lowers to `cudaLaunchKernel`: arguments are copied into a launch record, the grid and block dimensions and dynamic shared-memory size are attached, and the record is written into a user-mode command buffer for the stream. The call returns in a few microseconds of CPU time (commonly 3-5 µs) and does not wait for execution; no system call is made per launch. On the device the work distributor hands blocks to SMs as resources free up, and every thread computes its global index, for example `i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x`, and guards against `i >= n` because the grid is usually rounded up. A kernel can read and write global memory, shared memory and registers, call `__device__` functions, use atomics and warp intrinsics, and launch child grids (dynamic parallelism) on supported devices. It cannot call host functions, block on host events, allocate pinned host memory, or synchronize across blocks except through cooperative launch or global-memory atomics. Kernel arguments are passed by value through constant memory (limited to a few KB, 4 KB traditionally and 32 KB on recent toolkits, generation dependent), so large inputs are passed as device pointers. Errors inside a kernel (illegal address, misaligned access) are sticky: they poison the context and surface later at the next synchronizing call.',
    input: 'Grid and block dimensions, dynamic shared-memory size, a stream, and by-value arguments (scalars and device pointers).',
