    summary: 'A launch is a grid of thread blocks (CTAs), each block a group of up to 1024 threads that runs on one SM and shares that SM\'s shared memory and barrier. Blocks are scheduled independently in any order, which is what lets the same kernel scale from one SM to 132 and lets the hardware fill SMs as earlier blocks retire. Within a block, threads are further grouped into warps of 32 for execution.',
    purpose: 'Gives the programmer a scalable decomposition: cooperation (shared memory, barriers) inside a block, independence between blocks so the hardware can schedule freely.',
    mechanics: 'Dimensions are 1D, 2D or 3D for both grid and block; the block limit is 1024 threads on current NVIDIA GPUs, the grid x-dimension can reach `2^31 − 1` blocks. The work distributor assigns a whole block to an SM only when the SM has enough free registers, shared memory, warp slots and block slots for it (for example up to 32 resident blocks and 64 warps per SM on Ampere and Hopper, generation dependent). Once resident, a block stays on that SM until every thread exits; there is no migration or preemption at block granularity within a kernel. Threads in a block synchronize with `__syncthreads()` (a barrier over the block, `bar.sync` in PTX) and communicate through shared memory; the barrier is undefined if reached by only some threads. Blocks cannot synchronize with each other inside a kernel because not all blocks are guaranteed to be resident at once; grid-wide sync requires a cooperative launch (`cudaLaunchCooperativeKernel`) that checks all blocks fit simultaneously, or a separate kernel. Hopper adds thread block clusters: a group of up to 8 blocks (16 with a non-portable opt-in) co-scheduled on one GPC that can access each other\'s shared memory. Block size is chosen as a multiple of 32 (128 or 256 are common defaults) and balanced against registers per thread and shared memory per block so that enough blocks fit per SM; a grid-stride loop lets a fixed-size grid process any input size. Block-level parallelism also sets the tail: a kernel with 200 blocks on 132 SMs runs two waves with the second mostly empty.',
