    summary: 'On NVIDIA GPUs the hardware issues instructions per warp of 32 threads, not per thread. All 32 lanes execute the same instruction with a mask; when a branch condition differs across lanes, the warp runs each path in turn with the other lanes disabled. Warp-level primitives (shuffle, vote, match, reduce) let lanes exchange values through registers without shared memory.',
    purpose: 'Amortizes instruction fetch, decode and scheduling over 32 lanes so a single scheduler can drive wide datapaths, at the price of penalizing divergent control flow.',
    mechanics: 'A block of `n` threads is split into `ceil(n/32)` warps by consecutive `threadIdx` (lane id = `threadIdx.x % 32` for 1D blocks). Each SM sub-partition has a warp scheduler that picks an eligible warp per cycle and issues one instruction for it. On an `if` whose predicate varies within the warp, the compiler either predicates short bodies (both sides execute, results masked) or emits a branch; a divergent branch executes the taken and not-taken paths serially, so the cost is the sum of both paths, and reconverges afterward. Divergence only matters within a warp: different warps taking different paths cost nothing extra. Since Volta, each thread has its own program counter and call stack (independent thread scheduling), so lanes of a diverged warp can interleave and a thread can wait on another lane without deadlock; the cost model is the same but code can no longer assume implicit lockstep, which is why the `_sync` intrinsics take an explicit lane mask and `__syncwarp()` exists. `__shfl_sync(mask, v, srcLane)` reads another lane\'s register, `__ballot_sync` returns a 32-bit vote, `__match_any_sync` groups lanes by value, and `__reduce_add_sync` (compute capability 8.0+) performs a warp reduction in hardware; a full 32-lane reduction takes 5 shuffle steps. Memory instructions are also per warp: one load instruction produces one set of memory transactions for all active lanes, which is the basis of coalescing. Loops whose trip count varies per lane keep the warp alive until the longest lane finishes.',
