    summary: 'Global memory is moved in 32-byte sectors (grouped in 128-byte lines). When the 32 lanes of a warp load consecutive 4-byte words, the request is served by 4 sectors, the minimum. When lanes stride through memory, each lane touches a different sector and the same instruction moves 8x or 32x more bytes than it uses. Coalescing is the single most common reason a kernel reaches or misses HBM bandwidth.',
    purpose: 'Matches the per-warp access pattern to the width of the memory system so the bytes fetched are the bytes consumed.',
    mechanics: 'For each warp-wide load or store the load/store unit collects the active lanes\' addresses, groups them by 128-byte line and 32-byte sector, and issues one request per distinct sector (the L1 line is 128 bytes with four sectors; L2 and HBM traffic is in 32-byte sectors on Ampere and Hopper, generation dependent). Ideal: 32 lanes × 4 bytes = 128 bytes = 4 sectors, or 32 lanes × 16 bytes with `float4`/`LDG.128` = 512 bytes = 16 sectors in one instruction. Stride of 2 elements doubles the sectors per useful byte; a stride of 32 floats (column access of a row-major matrix) touches 32 sectors for 128 useful bytes, a 8x waste, and if the stride crosses lines it also thrashes L1. Misalignment shifts the window so 128 bytes span 5 sectors instead of 4. Array-of-structures layouts (`{x,y,z}` per element) make each lane read only part of a sector; structure-of-arrays (`x[]`, `y[]`, `z[]`) restores contiguity. Row-major matrices with a padded leading dimension keep row starts aligned. Vectorized loads require the pointer to be aligned to the vector width (16 bytes for `float4`); `__align__` and allocator alignment (the caching allocator rounds to 512 bytes) make this safe. Shared memory has a different rule (banks); transposes and strided patterns are usually staged through shared memory so that the global side stays coalesced. Coalescing does not require lane order to match address order, only that the set of addresses is dense.',
