    mechanics: 'For `C[M,N] = A[M,K] · B[K,N]` a block computes a `BM × BN` tile of C by iterating over K in chunks of `BK`: it copies `A[BM,BK]` and `B[BK,BN]` to shared memory, synchronizes, multiplies, synchronizes, and moves on. Global bytes per K-chunk are `(BM + BN)·BK·s` and FLOPs are `2·BM·BN·BK`, so intensity is `2·BM·BN / ((BM+BN)·s)` FLOP/byte, for example 128×128 tiles in BF16 give 64 FLOP/byte from global memory, and larger tiles raise it further at the cost of shared memory and fewer blocks per SM. Shared memory is organized in 32 banks of 4 bytes; a warp-wide access is conflict-free if lanes hit distinct banks or the same word (broadcast), and an `n`-way conflict serializes into `n` wavefronts. Column reads of a row-major tile whose row length is a multiple of 32 words hit the same bank; padding the row by one word or XOR-swizzling the layout (what CUTLASS and Triton do) removes the conflict. Double buffering allocates two tile buffers and overlaps the copy of chunk `k+1` (with `cp.async` or TMA) with the multiply of chunk `k`, hiding global latency behind compute; deeper pipelines use 3-4 stages. On tensor-core kernels the fragments are moved from shared memory to registers with `ldmatrix`, and the tile shape must match the `mma`/`wgmma` operand layout. Register tiling gives each thread a `TM × TN` micro-tile (for example 8×8) so shared-memory reads are also amortized; the limit is the register file (65,536 32-bit registers per SM on Ampere/Hopper, example). The per-SM shared-memory capacity (up to 227 KB per block on H100, 164 KB on A100, example) caps `BM·BK + BK·BN` times the stage count.',
