    summary: 'Occupancy is `resident warps / max warps per SM` (max 64 on Ampere and Hopper, example). It is limited by whichever per-SM resource runs out first: registers, shared memory, block slots or warp slots. More resident warps give the scheduler more candidates to issue from while others wait on memory, but a kernel with enough instruction-level parallelism per warp can saturate the SM at low occupancy.',
