    mechanics: 'Per SM (H100, example): 64 warp slots, 32 block slots, 65,536 32-bit registers, up to 228 KB shared memory. A block of 256 threads using 128 registers per thread needs `256 × 128 = 32,768` registers, so at most 2 such blocks (16 warps, 25% occupancy) fit by registers alone; the same block at 64 registers allows 4 blocks (50%). Registers are allocated in granules and rounded, and shared memory per block adds a second bound: `floor(228 KB / smem_per_block)` blocks. The runtime exposes the arithmetic through `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor` and the occupancy calculator in Nsight Compute. Theoretical occupancy is that upper bound; achieved occupancy (ncu `sm__warps_active`) is lower when blocks are unevenly sized, when the grid does not fill all SMs, or during the tail. Latency hiding works because each warp scheduler issues from a different warp while others wait; a global load takes hundreds of cycles, so at 1 outstanding load per warp roughly a dozen or more warps per scheduler are needed to keep the memory pipe busy, but a warp with 4 independent loads in flight needs a quarter as many. This is why hand-tuned GEMMs run at 25-50% occupancy with huge register tiles: they replace thread-level parallelism with ILP and shared-memory pipelining. `__launch_bounds__(maxThreads, minBlocks)` and `-maxrregcount` tell the compiler to cap registers to reach a target occupancy; if it caps too hard, values spill to local memory (HBM through L1/L2) and the kernel slows despite higher occupancy. Occupancy interacts with MPS and concurrent kernels: unused slots can be filled by another stream\'s blocks.',
