    mechanics: 'The block tile `BM × BN` is split across warps (or Hopper warpgroups of 4 warps) into warp tiles, and each warp issues tensor-core instructions: `mma.sync.m16n8k16` on Ampere (one warp, 16×8×16 per instruction) or `wgmma.mma_async.m64nNk16` on Hopper (one warpgroup, 64×N×16 with N up to 256, operands read from shared memory asynchronously). Typical tile shapes are 128×128×32 or 128×256×64 in BF16 (CUTLASS/cuBLAS, example) with 3-5 pipeline stages fed by `cp.async` or TMA. The grid has `ceil(M/BM) × ceil(N/BN)` blocks; with 132 SMs (H100, example) and one block per SM, 133 tiles take two waves, the second with a single block, so effective utilization is `tiles / (waves × SMs)`; this wave quantization is why throughput jumps at particular M and N. Split-K divides K across blocks and reduces partial sums (extra pass or atomics); stream-K assigns each block a contiguous range of MAC iterations across tiles so the work divides evenly. The epilogue applies α/β, bias, activation, dequantization or FP8 scaling while the tile is still in registers, then stores it, ideally with vectorized coalesced stores. Intensity for `[M,K]×[K,N]` is `2MNK / (2(MK + KN + MN))` FLOP/byte in BF16; for a weight matrix with `M` tokens or batch rows and `K,N ≫ M`, this is roughly `M`. On an H100 SXM (BF16 dense ≈ 989 TFLOP/s, HBM3 ≈ 3.35 TB/s) the ridge is ≈ 295 FLOP/byte, so decode with batch 8 sits at roughly 3% of peak and is purely a weight-streaming problem, while prefill with 4,096 tokens is compute-bound. Low-batch decode therefore uses GEMV-style or weight-only-quantized kernels that minimize bytes, and FP8/INT4 weights halve or quarter the time regardless of tensor-core rate. cuBLASLt picks a kernel per shape from a heuristic table; CUTLASS and Triton let engines tune tiles for their exact shapes; small-N shapes (LoRA, lm-head slices) and odd leading dimensions fall off the fast path.',
    input: 'Operands A `[M,K]`, B `[K,N]` (layouts, strides, dtype, scales), optional C and epilogue parameters.',
    output: 'C `[M,N]` written once from registers via the epilogue.',
    tensorShape: 'Block tile `BM×BN×BK` (for example 128×256×64) → warp/warpgroup tiles → `mma` fragments (16×8×16 per warp on Ampere; 64×N×16 per warpgroup on Hopper).',
    memory: 'Bytes = weights + activations + output; decode reads the full weight matrix per step no matter how small M is; shared-memory footprint = stages × (BM·BK + BK·BN) × dtype bytes.',
    compute: 'FLOPs = `2·M·N·K`; time ≈ `max(FLOPs / peak, bytes / bandwidth)` plus wave-quantization loss.',
    communication: 'Under tensor parallelism each GEMM is a shard followed by an all-reduce or all-gather; the GEMM tile shape and the NCCL chunking are tuned together for overlap.',
